+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Noise Sensitivity:** Texture analysis is highly sensitive to noise. The input dataset must be very clean; otherwise, texture analysis becomes difficult or impossible [S1].
+*   **Automatic Mode Risks:** The fully automatic method is barely controllable, has poor ability to emphasize determined features, and carries a high risk of wrong results or critical misinterpretation [S1].
+*   **Resolution Trade-off:** Texture analysis does not necessarily require full volume resolution. Lower resolution can be used to trade time for precision, allowing the method to handle larger datasets [S1].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Very high preprocessing time due to the computation of 20 texture metrics and potential clustering [S1]. Rendering time is comparable to other TFs if labels are used, but significantly higher if raw metrics are looked up (21 lookups vs 2) [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Very high. Requires storing up to 20 metric volumes plus 2 matrix volumes during preprocessing [S1]. During rendering, only the label volume is needed (1 additional volume), which does not necessarily need to be full size [S1].
-*   **Real-time Capability:** No. The preprocessing is too expensive for real-time interaction [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Can distinguish features with similar intensities but different textures (e.g., left vs. right features in Figure 6 of [S1], or cerebellum vs. brain in Figure 8d) [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Highly sensitive to noise; requires very clean datasets [S1]. The fully automatic mode is barely controllable and prone to misinterpretation [S1]. Semi-automatic manual weighting is difficult and time-consuming [S1].
-*   **Trade-offs:** Texture analysis does not require full volume resolution; lower resolution can be used to trade precision for speed [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Computing the 20 metrics and performing clustering is very expensive, leading to high preprocessing times [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Very high memory consumption during preprocessing. Up to 20 volumes are needed for the statistical metrics, plus one for GLCM and one for run-length matrices [S1]. During rendering, memory consumption is low (1 additional volume for labels), provided the automatic mode is not used [S1].
+*   **Real-time Capability:** No. It requires 2 3D texture lookups per sample if using labels (data + label) or 21 if using metrics (data + metrics) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Can distinguish features based on texture, separating materials with similar intensities but different microstructures [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** High computational cost, poor controllability in automatic mode, and high sensitivity to noise [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The tbTF was introduced in the context of comparing transfer function types for volume rendering, specifically citing [CR08] [S1]. It is part of a family of data feature-based classification methods that extend beyond scalar or gradient mapping. The method is particularly relevant in medical visualization where tissue texture differences are diagnostically important despite similar Hounsfield units or signal intensities [S1].
+The texture-based transfer function was introduced by [CR08] and is compared against other transfer function types (1D, gradient-based, curvature-based, size-based, distance-based) in a survey of transfer functions suitable for volume rendering [S1]. It is particularly relevant in medical visualization where distinguishing tissue types based on texture is critical [S1].
 
